   297	
   298	# Overview:
   299	#   Complete the blue diagonal backbones, then let every on-line trigger split into transmitted and reflected
   300	#   magenta branches that advance synchronously and stop when they meet other lines.
   301	# Input structure:
   302	#   The dominant color is background. One special non-background color forms endpoint pairs on 45-degree
   303	#   diagonals, and differently colored pixels may lie directly on those diagonals as triggers.
   304	# Rule:
   305	#   Fill each diagonal between the extreme same-color endpoints. Every trigger on such a diagonal creates
   306	#   four diagonal magenta branches: two continue along the backbone direction and two reflect into the
   307	#   perpendicular diagonal direction. These branches grow one step per round; a branch may pass through its
   308	#   own backbone, but it stops before any other existing line, before any previously painted magenta branch,
   309	#   and any cell claimed by multiple branches in the same round stays unchanged while all claimants stop.
   310	# Key insight:
   311	#   The output is determined by a synchronous light-path simulation, not by drawing independent rays in an
   312	#   arbitrary order. The visible length of each branch depends on which other blue or magenta line reaches a
   313	#   would-be next cell first.
   314	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   315	    return _combo_output(grid)[0]
